      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we note that $-\\frac{6}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a negative rational number that is not an integer.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$ contains the negative rational numbers. Therefore, $-\\frac{6}{9}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$ contains the nonnegative real numbers. Therefore, $-\\frac{6}{9}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$ contains the nonnegative rational numbers. Therefore, $-\\frac{6}{9}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}_0^{+}$ contains the nonnegative integers. Therefore, $-\\frac{6}{9}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{Z}_0^{+}$.\n\nTherefore, select $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$ only.",
